  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-142268",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To find the expression, let's apply each of the transformations in turn.\n\n1\\. Stretching the graph of $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis is equivalent to replacing every instance of $x$ with $3x.$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n  $$\n  y=f(3x).\n  $$\n\n2\\. Stretching the graph of $y=f(3x)$ by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis is equivalent to multiplying the function expression by $4.$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n  $$\n  y=4(f(3x))=4f(3x).\n  $$\n\n3\\. Translating the graph of $y=4f(3x)$ by $5$ units to the right is equivalent to replacing every instance of $x$ by $(x-5).$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n  $$\n  y=4f(3(x-5))=4f(3x-15).\n  $$\n\nTherefore, the final function expression is $y=4f(3x-15).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
